# Keeps the Docker build context small and, more importantly, keeps secrets out
# of the image. The Dockerfile does `COPY src ./src`, so anything under src/ is
# baked into a layer — being git-ignored does not help here.

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local development credentials. In deployment every value comes from platform
# environment variables via the ${VAR} placeholders in application.properties,
# so this file must never reach the image.
**/application-local.properties
**/application-*.local.properties
.env
.env.*
dev-env.ps1

# ── Build output ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The jar is produced by the `build` stage inside the image and copied with
# COPY --from=build, so the host's target/ is never read. Excluding it also stops
# a stale host build (different JDK, different profile) confusing the context.
target/

# ── VCS and tooling ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
*.iml
.idea/
.vscode/

# ── Noise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
*.md
